h a gap between them\n- Limited potential for strong hands (straights would need specific cards, no flush potential)\n\n**Current Situation:**\n- Opponent raised to 335 (from the big blind, this is a significant raise)\n- I need to call 335 to see the flop\n- Pot odds: Calling 335 to win approximately 670 (335 from opponent + my blinds)\n- I'm behind in the overall match (-450 cumulative)\n\n**Possible Actions:**\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Lose my blind investment but avoid risking more chips with a weak hand\n2. **Call (335)**: See the flop with a marginal hand, but pot odds aren't favorable given hand strength\n3. **Raise (670+)**: Aggressive play to represent strength, but risky with such a weak holding\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Extremely aggressive, but my hand doesn't justify this risk\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- 9-6 offsuit is one of the weaker starting hands in Texas Hold'em\n- Opponent's raise suggests strength or a steal attempt\n- I'm already behind in the match, but throwing away chips on weak hands will only make it worse\n- Even if I hit the flop, I'll likely only make one pair at best, which could easily be dominated\n- The pot odds (roughly 2:1) don't compensate for the low equity of this hand\n\nReason:\nWith 9♣ 6♥ offsuit, I have one of the weakest starting hands in poker. My opponent has shown aggression with a substantial raise. Even though I'm behind in the match, chasing with poor hands is not the way to recover. I should preserve my chips for better opportunities where I have a stronger chance of winning. Folding here is the disciplined play that gives me the best long-term expectation.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
